m. It's playable in heads-up poker but not strong enough to commit heavily pre-flop.\n\n**Position & Action:** My opponent checked (limped) from the small blind position. This shows weakness or a trapping strategy. Since they didn't raise, they likely don't have a premium hand.\n\n**Promising Moves:**\n1. **Check (0):** See a free flop with position. This is safe and reasonable given our marginal hand. We can reassess based on the flop.\n\n2. **Raise (200+):** Apply pressure and potentially take down the pot immediately. A raise to 200-400 chips represents a standard pre-flop raise (2-4x big blind). This could fold out weaker hands and establish control.\n\n3. **Fold (-1):** Too conservative. A7 with an Ace is playable heads-up, especially when opponent shows weakness by limping.\n\n4. **All-in (-2):** Extremely aggressive and unnecessary with 19,900 chips. Would be a massive overbet that risks our entire stack on a marginal hand.\n\n**Stack Considerations:** I'm slightly down 100 chips this hand (started 20,000, now have 19,900 from posting big blind). Overall I'm losing by 301 chips cumulatively, so I should be somewhat aggressive but not reckless.\n\nReason:\nI'll raise to 300 chips. My opponent's limp shows weakness, and with an Ace I have enough equity to apply pressure. A standard raise allows me to:\n- Build the pot when I likely have the best hand\n- Gain information about opponent's hand strength\n- Maintain fold equity\n- Not overcommit to a marginal holding\n\nThis balanced approach is better than passively checking, as it capitalizes on my opponent's apparent weakness while keeping the pot manageable.\n\nAnswer: 300"}
